ewcommand{	hesistitle}{Comparing Mutual Information in Finite Samples: Wald and Expanded Welch--Satterthwaite Tests}

ewcommand{	hesisauthor}{Daniel Zhao}

ewcommand{	hesisdegree}{Master of Computer Science}

ewcommand{	hesissupervisor}{Associate Professor Joseph T. Lizier}

ewcommand{	hesisschool}{School of Computer Science}

ewcommand{	hesisuniversity}{The University of Sydney}

ewcommand{	hesiscountry}{Australia}

ewcommand{	hesisdate}{2026}

\vspace{15mm}
{\normalsize
A thesis submitted in partial fulfilment of the requirements for the degree of\par
\vspace{3mm}
\textbf{\thesisdegree}\par}

Let \(P\) and \(Q\) be the joint probability distributions of \(X\) and
\(Y\) in the two populations. The null and alternative hypotheses are
\begin{equation}
  H_0:I(P)=I(Q),
  \qquad H_1:I(P)\ne I(Q).
  \label{eq:introduction-null}
\end{equation}
Under \(H_0\), the populations have equal MI. However, this does not mean
that their joint probability tables are the same, as two populations can
have equal MI while having different probability tables.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \begin{samepage}\textbf{Mathematical contribution.} The thesis derives how changing
  one observation affects the estimated MI variance, including the resulting
  changes in the row and column totals and in pointwise MI.
  \par
  \end{samepage}

Let \(P=(p_{ij})\) be the joint distribution of categorical variables
\(X\in\{1,\ldots,r\}\) and \(Y\in\{1,\ldots,c\}\).
The numbers of categories, \(r\) and \(c\), are their alphabet sizes. Write
\begin{equation}
  p_{i+}=\sum_{j=1}^{c}p_{ij},
  \qquad
  p_{+j}=\sum_{i=1}^{r}p_{ij}
\end{equation}
for the marginal probabilities, or row and column margins. The pointwise
mutual information (also called local MI) associated with cell \((i,j)\) is
\begin{equation}
  \pmi_P(i,j)
  =\log\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{i+}p_{+j}}\right).
  \label{eq:background-pmi}
\end{equation}
The denominator is the cell probability under independence with the same
margins. A positive value means that the cell occurs more often than
independence predicts, whereas a negative value means that it occurs less often.

Mutual information is the probability-weighted average of these pointwise
values \citep{shannon1948,bossomaier2016}:
\begin{equation}
  I(P)
  =\sum_{i=1}^{r}\sum_{j=1}^{c}p_{ij}\pmi_P(i,j).
  \label{eq:background-mi}
\end{equation}
Here the summations are nested: for each row \(i\), the inner sum runs over
all columns \(j\). Equivalently, the expression sums once over every cell in
the table and may be written with the shorthand \(\sum_{i,j}\).

Suppose a sample of size \(n\) produces counts \(N_{ij}\). The empirical cell
probabilities are
\begin{equation}
  \widehat p_{ij}=\frac{N_{ij}}{n}.
\end{equation}
The plug-in estimator inserts these observed relative frequencies into
Equation~\eqref{eq:background-mi}:
\begin{equation}
  \Ihat(P)
  =\sum_{i,j:\widehat p_{ij}>0}
  \widehat p_{ij}
  \log\left(
  \frac{\widehat p_{ij}}
       {\widehat p_{i+}\widehat p_{+j}}
  \right).
  \label{eq:plugin-mi-background}
\end{equation}
The zero-cell convention follows from
\(\lim_{x\downarrow0}x\log x=0\), so empty cells contribute no term to
the sum. When a positive-probability cell is unobserved, the empirical
table assigns it zero probability, contributing to sparse-sample estimation
error.

For a fixed positive \(r\times c\) support, the large-sample expansion of
the plug-in estimator's bias has a positive leading term:
\begin{equation}
  \Bias\{\Ihat(P)\}
  =\frac{(r-1)(c-1)}{2n}+O(n^{-2}).
  \label{eq:mi-leading-bias}
\end{equation}
The leading term grows with table size and shrinks as the sample grows,
although it does not describe every small or sparse sample. The result is closely related
to Miller's entropy correction \citep{miller1955}, and the same histogram-MI
term appears in \citet{moddemeijer1989}. Sparse sampling can produce further
error beyond this leading approximation \citep{paninski2003}.

Bias matters directly when comparing MI estimates from two populations. If
\(I(P)=I(Q)\) but \(n_P\ne n_Q\), the uncorrected difference has leading bias
\begin{equation}
  \frac{(r-1)(c-1)}{2}
  \left(\frac{1}{n_P}-\frac{1}{n_Q}\right).
  \label{eq:two-sample-mi-bias}
\end{equation}
For a \(2\times2\) table with \(n_P=50\) and \(n_Q=100\), Equation
\eqref{eq:two-sample-mi-bias} gives \(0.005\) nats despite equal population MI values. Chapter
\ref{chap:baselines} corrects this sample-size effect by subtracting
\((r-1)(c-1)/(2n)\) from each estimate.

\allowdisplaybreaks[0]
\section{Sampling variance of plug-in MI}
\label{sec:first-order-mi-background}

Using the entropy identity in Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-entropy-form}, MI can be
written directly in terms of the joint and marginal probabilities:
\begin{equation}
  I(P)
  =
  \sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\log p_{ij}
  -\sum_i p_{i+}\log p_{i+}
  -\sum_j p_{+j}\log p_{+j}.
  \label{eq:mi-probability-sums}
\end{equation}
Each sum contains the same function, \(f(u)=u\log u\). Expanding this
function lets us translate probability errors into changes in MI. The
derivative needed for its first-order Taylor
expansion follows directly from the product rule:
\begin{equation}
  f'(u)
  =\frac{d}{du}\{u\log u\}
  =\log u+u\frac{1}{u}
  =\log u+1.
  \label{eq:ulog-derivative-background}
\end{equation}
The general first-order Taylor formula is
\begin{equation}
  f(x)\approx f(a)+f'(a)(x-a),
  \label{eq:general-first-order-taylor-background}
\end{equation}
where \(a\) is the value around which the function is expanded and \(x-a\)
is the distance from that value. Here the new value is the estimated
probability \(x=\widehat u\), the expansion point is the population probability
\(a=u\), and \(f(t)=t\log t\). Therefore, \(x-a=\widehat u-u\), which is the
sampling error in the estimated probability. Substituting these quantities
into the general formula gives
\begin{equation}
  \widehat u\log\widehat u
  \approx
  \underbrace{u\log u}_{\text{population value}}
  +
  \underbrace{(\log u+1)(\widehat u-u)}_{\text{first-order change}}.
  \label{eq:first-order-taylor-background}
\end{equation}
Subtracting the population value from both sides makes the change explicit:
\begin{equation}
  \widehat u\log\widehat u-u\log u
  \approx
  (\log u+1)(\widehat u-u).
  \label{eq:ulog-change-background}
\end{equation}

After this rearrangement, the same cell error \(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij}\)
appears in the joint term, its
row-margin term and its column-margin term. Its combined coefficient is
\begin{align}
  &(\log p_{ij}+1)
  -(\log p_{i+}+1)
  -(\log p_{+j}+1)
  \notag\\
  &\qquad
  =\log\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{i+}p_{+j}}\right)-1
  \notag\\
  &\qquad
  =\pmi_P(i,j)-1.
  \label{eq:mi-error-cell-coefficient}
\end{align}
Therefore,
\begin{equation}
  \Ihat(P)-I(P)
  \approx
  \sum_{i,j}\{\pmi_P(i,j)-1\}(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij}).
  \label{eq:mi-error-before-constant-cancellation}
\end{equation}
The empirical and population cell probabilities both sum to one, so
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{i,j}(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij})
  =\sum_{i,j}\widehat p_{ij}-\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}
  =1-1=0.
  \label{eq:cell-errors-sum-zero}
\end{equation}
The constant \(-1\) in Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-error-cell-coefficient} therefore
contributes zero. Explicitly,
\begin{align}
  \Ihat(P)-I(P)
  &\approx
  \sum_{i,j}\{\pmi_P(i,j)-1\}(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij})
  \notag\\
  &=\sum_{i,j}\pmi_P(i,j)(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij})
    -\sum_{i,j}(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij})
  \notag\\
  &=\sum_{i,j}
  \pmi_P(i,j)(\widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij}).
  \label{eq:mi-error-from-cell-errors}
\end{align}
The complicated changes in the joint table and its two margins have therefore
reduced to a weighted sum of cell-probability errors. The weight for a cell is
its population pointwise MI.

Let \(Z_a=(X_a,Y_a)\) be observation \(a\). An empirical cell probability is
the proportion of observations in that cell, so
\begin{equation}
  \widehat p_{ij}-p_{ij}
  =
  \frac{1}{n}\sum_{a=1}^{n}
  \left\{\mathbf 1(Z_a=(i,j))-p_{ij}\right\}.
  \label{eq:cell-error-as-average}
\end{equation}
Substituting this expression into Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-error-from-cell-errors}
first gives
\begin{equation}
  \Ihat(P)-I(P)
  \approx
  \sum_{i,j}\pmi_P(i,j)
  \left[
    \frac{1}{n}\sum_{a=1}^{n}
    \{\mathbf 1(Z_a=(i,j))-p_{ij}\}
  \right].
  \label{eq:mi-error-double-sum-background}
\end{equation}
The finite sums can be exchanged, so the contribution from observation \(a\)
is gathered inside one pair of brackets:
\begin{align}
  \Ihat(P)-I(P)
  &\approx
  \frac{1}{n}\sum_{a=1}^{n}
  \left[
    \sum_{i,j}\pmi_P(i,j)\mathbf 1(Z_a=(i,j))
    -\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\pmi_P(i,j)
  \right]
  \notag\\
  &=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{a=1}^{n}
  \left\{\pmi_P(Z_a)-I(P)\right\}.
  \label{eq:mi-error-as-average-background}
\end{align}
The first sum inside the brackets selects the single cell containing
\(Z_a\), giving \(\pmi_P(Z_a)\). The second is the probability-weighted
average of all pointwise-MI values, which is \(I(P)\).

To first order, Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-error-as-average-background} expresses
the MI error as an average of \(n\) observation-level contributions.
The contribution of an observation in cell \((i,j)\) is simply that cell's
pointwise MI minus the population average, \(\pmi_P(i,j)-I(P)\). Its mean is
zero because
\begin{align}
  \E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)-I(P)\}
  &=\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\{\pmi_P(i,j)-I(P)\}
  \notag\\
  &=\underbrace{\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\pmi_P(i,j)}_{I(P)}
    -I(P)\underbrace{\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}}_{1}
  =0.
  \label{eq:mi-if-mean-zero-background}
\end{align}
The centred function \(\pmi_P(i,j)-I(P)\) is called the influence function
of MI\@. Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-error-as-average-background} uses it to
approximate the sampling error \citep{hampel1974,vanderVaart1998,kandasamy2015}.

Define the population variance of these centred contributions by
\begin{equation}
  V(P)
  =\Var_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)-I(P)\}
  =\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\{\pmi_P(i,j)-I(P)\}^2.
  \label{eq:mi-variance-background}
\end{equation}
Under independent sampling, the \(n\) terms in
Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-error-as-average-background} are independent and each
has variance \(V(P)\). The covariance between any two different observations
is therefore zero. Hence
\begin{align}
  \Var\{\Ihat(P)\}
  &\approx
  \Var\left\{
    \frac{1}{n}\sum_{a=1}^{n}
    \{\pmi_P(Z_a)-I(P)\}
  \right\}
  \notag\\
  &=\frac{1}{n^2}
    \sum_{a=1}^{n}\Var\{\pmi_P(Z_a)-I(P)\}
  \notag\\
  &=\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{a=1}^{n}V(P)
  =\frac{nV(P)}{n^2}
  =\frac{V(P)}{n}.
  \label{eq:mi-variance-derived-background}
\end{align}
Averaging \(n\) independent contributions therefore divides their variance
by \(n\). For dependent observations, covariance terms must also be included
in Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-variance-derived-background}, as studied by
\citet{moddemeijer1999}. The present experiment uses independent observations.

When \(V(P)>0\), the central limit theorem also gives
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\Ihat(P)-I(P)}{\sqrt{V(P)/n}}
  \xrightarrow{d}N(0,1).
  \label{eq:mi-clt-background}
\end{equation}
This gives a large-sample normal approximation for a fixed table with
positive population cell probabilities. The \(1/n\) leading bias from
Section~\ref{sec:background-mi-bias} decreases faster than the
\(1/\sqrt n\) sampling error, so it does not change this limiting
distribution.

\medskip
\thesisminiheading{The independence boundary.}
\nopagebreak[4]

\section{Approaches to testing MI differences}
\allowdisplaybreaks
\label{sec:welch-background}

A Wald test estimates a parameter difference, divides it by an estimated
standard error, and compares the result with a standard normal distribution
\citep{wald1943,vanderVaart1998}.
For MI estimated from independent samples from \(P\) and \(Q\), the
first-order variance contributions add:
\begin{equation}
  \Var\{\Ihat(P)-\Ihat(Q)\}
  \approx \frac{V(P)}{n_P}+\frac{V(Q)}{n_Q}.
  \label{eq:background-two-sample-variance}
\end{equation}
Each term is the sampling variance of one MI estimate, and independence of
the two samples makes their covariance zero. Replacing the population variances
by estimates gives the standardisation
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\Ihat(P)-\Ihat(Q)}
       {\sqrt{\Vhat(P)/n_P+\Vhat(Q)/n_Q}}
  \xrightarrow{d}N(0,1)
  \qquad\text{under }I(P)=I(Q).
  \label{eq:background-wald-standardisation}
\end{equation}
Here \(\Vhat(P)\) and \(\Vhat(Q)\) estimate the pointwise-MI variances
\(V(P)\) and \(V(Q)\). This limit assumes positive population cell
probabilities, positive pointwise-MI variances and increasing sample
sizes. Chapter~\ref{chap:baselines} applies the leading bias correction
to the numerator and specifies the full Normal Wald test.
With smaller samples, the MI difference may not follow the normal
approximation closely, and the estimated standard error can vary between
samples. The Wald test still uses the same standard normal distribution
cutoff, regardless of how much the estimated standard error varies.

This thesis adapts the Welch--Satterthwaite principle from comparing population
means to comparing population MI values. Welch's test allows the population
variances to differ and compares the standardised difference between two
sample means with a Student \(t\) distribution with \(\nu\) degrees of
freedom \citep{welch1947}. For two independent samples with sample means
\(\bar X_P,\bar X_Q\) and sample variances \(s_P^2,s_Q^2\), the statistic for
testing equal population means and the effective degrees of freedom are
\begin{align}
  T_{\mathrm{Welch}}
  &=\frac{\bar X_P-\bar X_Q}
          {\sqrt{s_P^2/n_P+s_Q^2/n_Q}},\\
  \nu
  &=\frac{\left(s_P^2/n_P+s_Q^2/n_Q\right)^2}
          {\dfrac{(s_P^2/n_P)^2}{n_P-1}
           +\dfrac{(s_Q^2/n_Q)^2}{n_Q-1}}.
  \label{eq:background-ordinary-welch}
\end{align}

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \includegraphics{focused_reference_distributions.pdf}
  \caption{Standard normal distribution and Student \(t\) distributions with 3, 10 and 30 degrees of freedom.}
  \label{fig:normal-student-t}
\end{figure}

Hutcheson's test is a direct information-theoretic Welch predecessor. It
compares two Shannon diversity estimates with an estimated variance and a
Student \(t\) distribution, using
\begin{equation}
  \nu_H=
  \frac{(s_{H,P}^2+s_{H,Q}^2)^2}
       {(s_{H,P}^2)^2/n_P+(s_{H,Q}^2)^2/n_Q}
  \label{eq:hutcheson-df}
\end{equation}
as its effective degrees of freedom \citep{hutcheson1970}. Here each of
\(s_{H,P}^2\) and \(s_{H,Q}^2\) is the estimated sampling variance of the
corresponding entropy estimator \(\widehat H\), and each variance component
is assigned its sample size, not \(n_i-1\).
Hutcheson's rule assigns degrees of freedom from sample sizes. Expanded
Welch instead calculates how the estimated MI variance changes with the
table, including the effects of a cell change on its row and column totals.

The population parameter is the signed difference
\begin{equation}
  \Delta=I(P)-I(Q).
  \label{eq:delta-population}
\end{equation}
The principal test is two-sided:
\begin{equation}
  H_0:\Delta=0
  \quad\text{against}\quad
  H_1:\Delta\ne0.
  \label{eq:two-sample-null}
\end{equation}

\begin{samepage}
\begin{assumption}[Sampling]
Observations are independent and identically distributed within each
population, and the two population samples are independent.
\end{assumption}
\end{samepage}

For the first table, define empirical pointwise MI by
\begin{equation}
  \widehat\pmi_P(i,j)
  =\log\left(
  \frac{\widehat p_{ij}}
       {\widehat p_{i+}\widehat p_{+j}}
  \right)
  \qquad\text{when }\widehat p_{ij}>0.
  \label{eq:empirical-pmi}
\end{equation}
The plug-in MI estimate is
\begin{equation}
  \Ihat(P)
  =\sum_{i,j:\widehat p_{ij}>0}
  \widehat p_{ij}\widehat\pmi_P(i,j).
  \label{eq:plugin-mi}
\end{equation}
The same calculation gives \(\Ihat(Q)\).

The leading fixed-support MI bias appears in
Equation~\eqref{eq:mi-leading-bias} \citep{miller1955,moddemeijer1989}. Subtracting it
from each plug-in estimate gives the estimated effect:
\begin{equation}
  \Deltahat
  =\left\{\Ihat(P)-\frac{(r-1)(c-1)}{2n_P}\right\}
  -\left\{\Ihat(Q)-\frac{(r-1)(c-1)}{2n_Q}\right\}.
  \label{eq:bias-corrected-difference}
\end{equation}
The two terms in braces are the bias-corrected MI estimates for the separate
populations.
The correction uses the declared row and column categories because every
population cell has positive probability under the model, even when some
cells are unobserved in a sample. Corrected estimates can be negative;
those values are retained when calculating the difference.
When \(n_P=n_Q\), the two corrections cancel, so \(\Deltahat\) is exactly
the raw plug-in MI difference. With unequal sample sizes, their difference
removes the leading unequal-sample bias described in
Section~\ref{sec:background-mi-bias}. A correction that estimates support
from occupied cells would be a different estimator.

The observed table estimates \(V(P)\) by
\begin{equation}
  \Vhat(P)
  =\sum_{i,j:\widehat p_{ij}>0}
  \widehat p_{ij}
  \{\widehat\pmi_P(i,j)-\Ihat(P)\}^2.
  \label{eq:estimated-mi-variance}
\end{equation}
Define \(\Vhat(Q)\) analogously.

Because the two samples are independent, their estimated variance
contributions add:
\begin{equation}
  \SEhat^2
  =\frac{\Vhat(P)}{n_P}
  +\frac{\Vhat(Q)}{n_Q}.
  \label{eq:shared-se}
\end{equation}
The common standardised statistic is
\begin{equation}
  T=\frac{\Deltahat}{\SEhat}.
  \label{eq:shared-t}
\end{equation}
The sign of \(T\) indicates whether \(P\) or \(Q\) has the larger
corrected MI estimate, while its magnitude measures the difference
relative to its estimated standard error. Under \(H_0\), we compare \(T\) with a reference distribution
centred at zero.
Equations~\eqref{eq:bias-corrected-difference}--\eqref{eq:shared-t} are shared
by Normal Wald and Expanded Welch. Their difference lies in the reference
distribution, not the effect estimate or plug-in standard error.

When both sample sizes grow while the table dimensions and positive
population probabilities stay fixed, the statistic approaches a standard
normal distribution under equal population MI
\citep{wald1943,vanderVaart1998}:
\begin{equation}
  T\xrightarrow{d}N(0,1)
  \qquad\text{under }H_0.
\end{equation}
The two-sided Normal Wald p-value is
\begin{equation}
  p_{\mathrm{Wald}}
  =2\{1-\Phi(|T|)\},
  \label{eq:wald-pvalue}
\end{equation}
where \(\Phi\) is the standard normal distribution function.

The Welch--Satterthwaite equation combines the two estimated MI variance
contributions
\citep{satterthwaite1946,welch1947}:
\begin{equation}
  \nu
  =
  \frac{
  \left\{\Vhat(P)/n_P+\Vhat(Q)/n_Q\right\}^2
  }{
  \left\{\Vhat(P)/n_P\right\}^2/\nu_P
  +\left\{\Vhat(Q)/n_Q\right\}^2/\nu_Q
  }.
  \label{eq:ws-general}
\end{equation}
The terms \(\Vhat(P)/n_P\) and \(\Vhat(Q)/n_Q\) are the estimated variance
contributions used to calculate the standard error for both Normal Wald and
Expanded Welch in Chapter~\ref{chap:baselines}. The new variables being
introduced are the component degrees of freedom \(\nu_P\) and \(\nu_Q\).
They describe how much each
estimated MI variance varies between samples relative to its mean.

Satterthwaite approximates the nonnegative variance estimate \(\Vhat(P)\) by
the scaled chi-squared variable
\begin{equation}
  \E\{\Vhat(P)\}
  \frac{\chi^2_{\nu_P}}{\nu_P}.
  \label{eq:scaled-chi-working}
\end{equation}
The scale and degrees of freedom are chosen to match the mean and variance
of \(\Vhat(P)\).

For a chi-squared variable with \(\nu_P\) degrees of freedom,
\begin{equation}
  \E(\chi^2_{\nu_P})=\nu_P,
  \qquad
  \Var(\chi^2_{\nu_P})=2\nu_P.
\end{equation}
The scaling in Equation~\eqref{eq:scaled-chi-working} therefore matches the
mean automatically:
\begin{equation}
  \E\left[
  \E\{\Vhat(P)\}\frac{\chi^2_{\nu_P}}{\nu_P}
  \right]
  =\E\{\Vhat(P)\}.
\end{equation}
Its variance is
\begin{align}
  \Var\left[
  \E\{\Vhat(P)\}\frac{\chi^2_{\nu_P}}{\nu_P}
  \right]
  &=
  \frac{[\E\{\Vhat(P)\}]^2}{\nu_P^2}
  \Var(\chi^2_{\nu_P})\\
  &=
  \frac{2[\E\{\Vhat(P)\}]^2}{\nu_P}.
\end{align}
Matching this quantity to \(\Var\{\Vhat(P)\}\) gives
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \nu_P
  =
  \frac{2[\E\{\Vhat(P)\}]^2}
       {\Var\{\Vhat(P)\}}
  }.
  \label{eq:component-df-moments}
\end{equation}
\begin{samepage}%
The numerator depends on the mean of the estimated MI variance; the
denominator measures how much that estimate fluctuates between samples.
To first order, \(\E\{\Vhat(P)\}\approx V(P)\), so the remaining task is
to calculate \(\Var\{\Vhat(P)\}\).
\par
\end{samepage}

For an arbitrary cell \((i,j)\),
\begin{equation}
  p_{ij}(\varepsilon)
  =(1-\varepsilon)p_{ij}
  +\varepsilon\mathbf 1\{i=x,j=y\}.
\end{equation}
The joint and marginal probability derivatives at the original distribution
are
\begin{align}
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d p_{ij}(\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}
  &=\mathbf 1\{i=x,j=y\}-p_{ij},
  \label{eq:joint-perturbation-derivative}\\
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d p_{i+}(\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}
  &=\mathbf 1\{i=x\}-p_{i+},\\
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d p_{+j}(\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}
  &=\mathbf 1\{j=y\}-p_{+j}.
\end{align}
The derivatives of the joint probabilities sum to zero because
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{i,j}
  \{\mathbf 1(i=x,j=y)-p_{ij}\}
  =1-1=0.
\end{equation}

Along the perturbation path, pointwise MI is
\begin{equation}
  \pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j)
  =\log p_{ij}(\varepsilon)
  -\log p_{i+}(\varepsilon)
  -\log p_{+j}(\varepsilon).
\end{equation}
\begin{samepage}%
For each logarithm, the derivative is the probability derivative divided by
the probability. Substituting the joint and marginal derivatives gives
\begin{align}
  \left.
  \frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j)
  \right|_{0}
  &={}
  \frac{\mathbf 1\{i=x,j=y\}-p_{ij}}{p_{ij}}
  -\frac{\mathbf 1\{i=x\}-p_{i+}}{p_{i+}}
  -\frac{\mathbf 1\{j=y\}-p_{+j}}{p_{+j}}
  \notag\\*
  &={}
  \frac{\mathbf 1\{i=x,j=y\}}{p_{ij}}
  -\frac{\mathbf 1\{i=x\}}{p_{i+}}
  -\frac{\mathbf 1\{j=y\}}{p_{+j}}
  +1.
  \label{eq:pmi-derivative}
\end{align}
The joint term supplies \(-1\), while subtracting the row and column
terms supplies \(+1\) each. Their sum is the final \(+1\).
\par
\end{samepage}

MI along the path is
\begin{equation}
  I(P_\varepsilon)
  =\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}(\varepsilon)
  \pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j).
\end{equation}

The product rule separates its derivative into the change in probability
weights and the change in pointwise-MI values:
\begin{align}
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d I(P_\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}
  ={}&
  \sum_{i,j}
  \{\mathbf 1(i=x,j=y)-p_{ij}\}\pmi_P(i,j)
  \notag\\
  &+
  \sum_{i,j}p_{ij}
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d\pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j)}
  {\mathrm d\varepsilon}\right|_{0}.
\end{align}
The first sum is \(\pmi_P(x,y)-I(P)\). In the second sum, the selected-cell
term contributes \(p_{xy}/p_{xy}=1\). The row term contributes
\(-\sum_j p_{xj}/p_{x+}=-1\); the column term likewise contributes \(-1\);
and the constant contributes \(\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}=1\). These four
contributions cancel. Hence
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d I(P_\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}
  =\pmi_P(x,y)-I(P)
  }.
  \label{eq:mi-derivative}
\end{equation}
This is the centred pointwise-MI influence function from
Section~\ref{sec:first-order-mi-background}.

The target is the first-order change in
\begin{equation}
  V(P)
  =\Var_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\}.
\end{equation}
Write this variance in second-moment form:
\begin{equation}
  V(P)=\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)^2\}-I(P)^2.
  \label{eq:v-second-moment}
\end{equation}
Define the observation-level variance sensitivity by
\begin{equation}
  g_P(x,y)
  =
  \left.
  \frac{\mathrm d V(P_\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}.
  \label{eq:g-definition}
\end{equation}
Differentiating Equation~\eqref{eq:v-second-moment} gives
\begin{equation}
  g_P(x,y)
  =
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \E_{P_\varepsilon}\{\pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(X,Y)^2\}
  \right|_{0}
  -2I(P)
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d I(P_\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}.
  \label{eq:g-decomposition}
\end{equation}

It remains to differentiate
\begin{equation}
  \E_{P_\varepsilon}\{\pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(X,Y)^2\}
  =\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}(\varepsilon)
  \pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j)^2.
\end{equation}
The product rule gives
\begin{align}
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \E_{P_\varepsilon}\{\pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(X,Y)^2\}
  \right|_{0}
  ={}&
  \sum_{i,j}
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d p_{ij}(\varepsilon)}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
  \right|_{0}
  \pmi_P(i,j)^2
  \notag\\
  &+
  2\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\pmi_P(i,j)
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d\pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j)}
  {\mathrm d\varepsilon}\right|_{0}.
  \label{eq:m2-product-rule}
\end{align}
The first sum is
\begin{equation}
  \pmi_P(x,y)^2-\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)^2\}.
  \label{eq:m2-weight-term}
\end{equation}

For the second sum, define the probability-weighted row and column means
\begin{align}
  \E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\mid X=x\}
  &=\frac{\sum_j p_{xj}\pmi_P(x,j)}{p_{x+}},\\
  \E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\mid Y=y\}
  &=\frac{\sum_i p_{iy}\pmi_P(i,y)}{p_{+y}}.
\end{align}
Substitution of Equation~\eqref{eq:pmi-derivative} gives
\begin{align}
  &\sum_{i,j}p_{ij}\pmi_P(i,j)
  \left.\frac{\mathrm d\pmi_{P_\varepsilon}(i,j)}
  {\mathrm d\varepsilon}\right|_{0}
  \notag\\
  &\quad=
  \pmi_P(x,y)
  -\frac{\sum_j p_{xj}\pmi_P(x,j)}{p_{x+}}
  -\frac{\sum_i p_{iy}\pmi_P(i,y)}{p_{+y}}
  +I(P)
  \notag\\
  &\quad=
  \pmi_P(x,y)
  -\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\mid X=x\}
  -\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\mid Y=y\}
  +I(P).
  \label{eq:m2-score-term}
\end{align}

Combining Equations~\eqref{eq:mi-derivative},
\eqref{eq:g-decomposition}, \eqref{eq:m2-weight-term}, and
\eqref{eq:m2-score-term} gives the variance sensitivity. To see how its
squared term arises, combine the weight-change term in
Equation~\eqref{eq:m2-weight-term} with the derivative of \(-I(P)^2\):
\begin{align*}
 &\pmi_P(x,y)^2-\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)^2\}
       -2I(P)\{\pmi_P(x,y)-I(P)\}\\*
 &\quad=\pmi_P(x,y)^2-\{V(P)+I(P)^2\}
       -2I(P)\pmi_P(x,y)+2I(P)^2\\*
 &\quad=\pmi_P(x,y)^2-2I(P)\pmi_P(x,y)+I(P)^2-V(P)\\*
 &\quad=\{\pmi_P(x,y)-I(P)\}^2-V(P).
\end{align*}
\begin{samepage}%
The second line uses
\(\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)^2\}=V(P)+I(P)^2\); the last line completes the
square. Adding twice the expression in Equation~\eqref{eq:m2-score-term}
gives
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
g_P(x,y)
={}&\{\pmi_P(x,y)-I(P)\}^2-V(P)\\
&+2\bigl[
\pmi_P(x,y)
-\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\mid X=x\}\\
&\hspace{18mm}
-\E_P\{\pmi_P(X,Y)\mid Y=y\}
+I(P)
\bigr].
\end{aligned}
}
\label{eq:g-final}
\end{equation}
The first line is the change if the pointwise-MI values were held fixed. The
bracket accounts for the fact that they change too: each pointwise-MI value
uses a cell probability and its row and column totals. The conditional means
collect the effects of those changing totals. Both parts are needed to
describe how the estimated variance varies across samples.
\par
\end{samepage}

Define the population variability of the observation-level sensitivities by
\begin{equation}
  \tau^2(P)=\Var_P\{g_P(X,Y)\}.
  \label{eq:tau-definition}
\end{equation}
The observation-level sensitivities \(g_P(x,y)\) have a probability-weighted
mean of zero. Averaging the one-cell perturbation directions leaves the
population table unchanged:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{x,y}p_{xy}\{\delta_{(x,y)}-P\}=P-P=0.
\end{equation}
This weighted cancellation also holds after exact plug-in substitution.
Because the first derivative is linear in the direction of change, the
weighted average of the one-cell derivatives is the derivative in the
weighted-average direction:
\begin{align}
  \E_P\{g_P(X,Y)\}
  &=\sum_{x,y}p_{xy}
    \left.\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
    V\{(1-\varepsilon)P+\varepsilon\delta_{(x,y)}\}
    \right|_{0}\\
  &=\left.\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}
    V\left\{P+\varepsilon
    \sum_{x,y}p_{xy}\{\delta_{(x,y)}-P\}\right\}
    \right|_{0}\\
  &=\left.\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\varepsilon}V(P)
    \right|_{0}=0.
\end{align}
Their variation determines how much the estimated MI variance changes
between samples.

An observation in cell \(Z_a^{(P)}\) can be represented by a table that places
probability one in that cell, denoted \(\delta_{Z_a^{(P)}}\). Relative to
\(P\), that observation contributes the table change
\(\delta_{Z_a^{(P)}}-P\). The empirical table averages these one-observation
tables, so its difference from \(P\) is
\begin{equation}
  \widehat P-P
  =\frac{1}{n_P}\sum_{a=1}^{n_P}
  \left\{\delta_{Z_a^{(P)}}-P\right\}.
\end{equation}
The plug-in \(\Vhat(P)\) applies the variance calculation to this empirical
table. Equation~\eqref{eq:g-definition} gives its first-order change for
one observation's direction. Because a first-order change is linear in the
table error \citep[Chapter~20]{vanderVaart1998}, the changes from the
observations can be averaged:
\begin{equation}
  \Vhat(P)-V(P)
  \approx
  \frac{1}{n_P}
  \sum_{a=1}^{n_P}g_P(Z_a^{(P)}).
  \label{eq:vhat-linearization}
\end{equation}
The observations are independent, so
\begin{align}
  \Var\{\Vhat(P)\}
  &\approx
  \frac{1}{n_P^2}
  \sum_{a=1}^{n_P}
  \Var_P\{g_P(Z_a^{(P)})\}\\*
  &=\frac{\tau^2(P)}{n_P}.
  \label{eq:vhat-sampling-variance}
\end{align}
Substitution into Equation~\eqref{eq:component-df-moments} gives the
population component degree of freedom
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \nu_P
  \approx
  \frac{2n_PV(P)^2}{\tau^2(P)}
  }.
  \label{eq:population-component-df}
\end{equation}
To interpret Equation~\eqref{eq:population-component-df}, divide the
sampling variance of \(\Vhat(P)\) by \(V(P)^2\), the square of the
population pointwise-MI variance:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\Var\{\Vhat(P)\}}{V(P)^2}
  \approx \frac{\tau^2(P)}{n_PV(P)^2},
\end{equation}
so the component degrees of freedom are approximately twice the reciprocal of
this relative variance. When the estimate fluctuates little compared with
the size of \(V(P)\), the degrees of freedom are large and the Student
\(t\) distribution is close to the standard normal distribution, whereas
greater relative fluctuation gives fewer degrees of freedom and a larger rejection
cutoff.
Positive \(V(P)\) does not guarantee positive \(\tau^2(P)\): table changes
can have no first-order effect on \(V(P)\). In that case, describing the
sampling variation of \(\Vhat(P)\) requires a higher-order approximation.

The observed table replaces every population quantity in Equation
\eqref{eq:g-final} by its empirical counterpart. The sums below are over
occupied cells, because pointwise MI is undefined at an empirical zero. For
each occupied cell,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\widehat g_P(i,j)
={}&\{\widehat\pmi_P(i,j)-\Ihat(P)\}^2-\Vhat(P)\\
&+2\left[
\widehat\pmi_P(i,j)
-\frac{\sum_{j'}\widehat p_{ij'}\widehat\pmi_P(i,j')}
       {\widehat p_{i+}}
-\frac{\sum_{i'}\widehat p_{i'j}\widehat\pmi_P(i',j)}
       {\widehat p_{+j}}
+\Ihat(P)
\right].
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:g-hat}
\end{equation}
In all weighted sums, an empty cell contributes zero; no logarithm or
sensitivity needs to be evaluated there. The empirical variance is centred
explicitly:
\begin{equation}
  \widehat\tau^2(P)
  =\sum_{i,j}\widehat p_{ij}
  \left\{\widehat g_P(i,j)
  -\sum_{k,l}\widehat p_{kl}\widehat g_P(k,l)\right\}^2.
  \label{eq:tau-hat}
\end{equation}
The estimated component degree of freedom is
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \widehat\nu_P
  =\frac{2n_P\Vhat(P)^2}{\widehat\tau^2(P)}
  }.
  \label{eq:nu-p-hat}
\end{equation}
Repeating the same calculation for the second table gives
\(\widehat\nu_Q\).

Substituting the two variance contributions and component degrees of freedom
into Equation~\eqref{eq:ws-general} gives
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \widehat\nu_{\mathrm{expanded}}
  =
  \frac{
  \left\{\Vhat(P)/n_P+\Vhat(Q)/n_Q\right\}^2
  }{
  \left\{\Vhat(P)/n_P\right\}^2/\widehat\nu_P
  +
  \left\{\Vhat(Q)/n_Q\right\}^2/\widehat\nu_Q
  }
  }.
  \label{eq:expanded-df}
\end{equation}
The expanded two-sided p-value is
\begin{equation}
  p_{\mathrm{expanded}}
  =2\left\{1-F_t(|T|;\widehat\nu_{\mathrm{expanded}})\right\},
  \label{eq:expanded-p}
\end{equation}
where \(F_t(x;\nu)\) denotes the cumulative distribution function of a
Student \(t\) distribution with \(\nu\) degrees of freedom, evaluated at \(x\).

\pagebreak[3]
\medskip
\thesisminiheading{Operational algorithm and complexity.}
\label{sec:expanded-complexity}

Consider a population table that
approaches independence while its row and column margins stay fixed. At
independence, MI is zero and its first-order change is also zero. The
leading changes in MI, the pointwise-MI variance \(V(P)\), and the variance
sensitivity \(\tau^2(P)\) can therefore be compared using a second-order
expansion. Appendix~\ref{sec:fixed-margin-local-derivation} gives the
calculation and its fixed-margin assumptions. Close to independence on
that path, it gives
\begin{equation}
 V(P)\approx 2I(P),
 \qquad \tau^2(P)\approx4V(P).
 \label{eq:near-independent-moments}
\end{equation}
Substituting these local approximations into
the component degrees-of-freedom formula gives
\begin{equation}
 \nu_P\approx\frac{2n_PV(P)^2}{\tau^2(P)}
 \approx\frac{n_PV(P)}{2}
 \approx n_PI(P).
 \label{eq:near-independent-df}
\end{equation}
This population approximation describes the estimated degrees of freedom
when sampling errors are small enough for the first-order calculation to
be accurate. Smaller population MI then gives fewer degrees of freedom
and a larger cutoff from the Student \(t\) distribution.

\begin{samepage}
\medskip
\thesisminiheading{Theoretical status and validity conditions.}

At each table size, we compare three patterns of category frequencies,
called margin regimes. Uniform margins assign probability \(1/k\) to
every category. For uneven margins, the first category has probability
\(\rho\), and the remaining probability \(1-\rho\) is shared equally
among the other \(k-1\) categories. We write this vector as
\begin{equation}
 m_k(\rho)=\left(\rho,\frac{1-\rho}{k-1},\ldots,
                   \frac{1-\rho}{k-1}\right).
 \label{eq:skew-margin}
\end{equation}
For example, assigning probability 0.8 to the first of four categories gives
\begin{equation*}
 m_4(0.8)=\left(0.8,\frac{0.2}{3},\frac{0.2}{3},\frac{0.2}{3}\right).
\end{equation*}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Row and column margins for each population.}
\label{tab:margin-profiles}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Regime & \(P\): rows and columns & \(Q\): rows and columns\\
\midrule
Uniform & \((1/k,\ldots,1/k)\) & \((1/k,\ldots,1/k)\)\\
Same skew & \(m_k(0.8)\) & \(m_k(0.8)\)\\
Different skew & \(m_k(0.7)\) & \(m_k(0.8)\)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The observation-level sensitivity derived in Chapter~\ref{chap:expanded}
describes how the estimated MI variance responds to changes in the table.
Let \(\tau^2(P)\) be its population variance. The first-order predictions are
\begin{equation}
 \E\{\Vhat(P)\}\approx V(P),\qquad
 \Var\{\Vhat(P)\}\approx\frac{\tau^2(P)}{n_P}.
 \label{eq:redesign-moment-approximations}
\end{equation}
We compare \(V(P)\) with the observed mean of \(\Vhat(P)\), and
\(\tau^2(P)/n_P\) with its observed sampling variance. We also average the sample-based
prediction \(\widehat\tau^2(P)/n_P\) across the repeated tables. Comparing
this average with the same observed sampling variance shows the effect of
estimating the sensitivity from each sample.

