\begin{samepage}
For each curve, we hold \(I(P)=0.02\) nats and increase \(I(Q)\).
Let \(h\) be the number of fitted null SDs separating the population MI
values. The populations satisfy
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 I(P)&=0.02,\qquad I(Q)=0.02+h\sigma_0,\\
 h&\in\{0,0.5,1,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.5,4\},\\
 \Delta&=I(P)-I(Q)=-h\sigma_0.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:design-focused-populations}
\end{equation}
\end{samepage}
Thus \(h=0\) gives equal MI, while positive \(h\) gives larger MI in
\(Q\) and a negative signed difference. For example, at C, two null SDs
are approximately \(2(0.003175)=0.00635\) nats. The corresponding
alternative has \(I(P)=0.02\), \(I(Q)\simeq0.02635\) and
\(\Delta\simeq-0.00635\) nats. The fitted SD and the resulting population
tables stay fixed during evaluation. Appendix~\ref{app:construction}
gives their construction and the fixed SD values.

