For positive component degrees of freedom, substituting
Equation~\eqref{eq:nu-p-hat} and its \(Q\) analogue into
Equation~\eqref{eq:expanded-df} gives the equivalent combined form
\begin{equation}
  \widehat\nu_{\mathrm{expanded}}
  =
  \frac{
  \left\{\Vhat(P)/n_P+\Vhat(Q)/n_Q\right\}^2
  }{
  \widehat\tau^2(P)/(2n_P^3)
  +
  \widehat\tau^2(Q)/(2n_Q^3)
  }.
  \label{eq:expanded-df-direct}
\end{equation}
This direct form remains defined when one table has
\(\Vhat=\widehat\tau^2=0\) and the other gives a positive denominator.
The zero contribution drops out, leaving the other table's component degrees
of freedom. The implementation nevertheless requires both components to have
positive, finite degrees of freedom, so it records this case as invalid.

\section{Component moment targets and uncertainty}
\label{sec:moment-target-details}
\label{sec:moment-uncertainty}

The first comparison in Chapter~\ref{chap:design} measures the mean and
sampling variance of \(\Vhat(P)\) separately. Expectations and variances
refer to repeated samples from the same fixed population at the same
sample size. Substituting the predictions in
Equation~\eqref{eq:redesign-moment-approximations} into the moment-matching
formula gives
\begin{equation}
 \nu_{P,\mathrm{FO}}=\frac{2n_PV(P)^2}{\tau^2(P)}.
 \label{eq:redesign-first-order-df}
\end{equation}
The subscript FO identifies the population first-order formula from
Chapter~\ref{chap:expanded}. The sampling-moment target instead uses
the actual mean and variance of the estimator:
\begin{equation}
 \nu_{P,\mathrm{mom}}
 =\frac{2\{\E\Vhat(P)\}^2}{\Var\{\Vhat(P)\}}.
 \label{eq:redesign-moment-df}
\end{equation}
The assumptions in Chapter~\ref{chap:expanded} ensure positive population
variance quantities for the tested populations. The two degrees-of-freedom
values can nevertheless differ at finite sample sizes. Dividing their
definitions before applying either approximation gives the exact identity
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\nu_{P,\mathrm{FO}}}{\nu_{P,\mathrm{mom}}}
 =\frac{\left\{V(P)/\E\Vhat(P)\right\}^{2}}
        {\{\tau^2(P)/n_P\}/\Var\{\Vhat(P)\}}.
 \label{eq:redesign-df-decomposition}
\end{equation}
The numerator is the reciprocal of the squared mean ratio in
Figure~\ref{fig:redesign-moments}; the denominator is the middle-row
predicted / observed sampling-variance ratio. A mean estimate above \(V(P)\) lowers the first-order
degrees of freedom relative to the moment target when the variance
prediction is otherwise accurate. The same calculations are made
separately for \(Q\).

The delta method propagates errors in the estimated mean and variance
jointly. Their covariance matters because both use the same repetitions.
Define
\begin{equation}
 U_s=X_s-\bar X,\qquad W_s=(X_s-\bar X)^2-S_X^2.
\end{equation}
The first-order contribution to the target's estimation error is
\begin{equation}
 Z_s=\frac{4\bar X}{S_X^2}U_s
       -\frac{2\bar X^2}{(S_X^2)^2}W_s.
 \label{eq:moment-df-mc-contribution}
\end{equation}
Its sample SD divided by \(\sqrt M\) estimates the Monte Carlo standard
error. Calculating the SD after combining the two terms retains the
mean--variance covariance. The experimental supplement gives the Taylor
expansion producing these coefficients.

